ted. Thinking about \"External Fire\", I'd expect more uniform charring. However, here, the connection points seem to be the epicenter of the most intense deformation. The damage is clearly concentrated at the splice points.\n\nLet's summarize my visual description: In the context image, I have that pronounced spring-back. The copper strands have that reddish-orange hue, a clear indicator of cuprous oxide. The green connection caps are melted and charred, especially where the wires enter, suggesting internal heat generation. The insulation is heavily carbonized, too. From a causal perspective, that cuprous oxide points to high-temperature oxidation in a restricted oxygen environment, exactly what you get with internal resistive heating. The spring-back? Thermal stress, repeated expansion and contraction – a direct result of poor contact. I'm focusing on the splice points themselves and the damage is concentrated there.\n\nMy final verdict? **Poor Contact (접촉 불량)**. My confidence level is high, around 90%, because I see the unmistakable combo of cuprous oxide and spring-back. It is the same as the Korean description, and my schema follows.", "will_print_thinking": true}, "timestamp": 1767661257799}
