ddish coloration points to localized, internal high temperatures. The spring-back suggests thermal stress from repeated heating/cooling or a sudden high-heat event from within. Now, if this were an external fire, I'd expect to see black cupric oxide (CuO) due to more abundant oxygen. The insulation would melt from the outside in. I'd be looking for a more uniform charring without this explosive, internal-out appearance.\n\nMy final verdict is that this was a poor contact. Based on what I have observed, I'm at 90% confidence that this was caused by internal heating from poor contact within the splice – the reddish cuprous oxide is the smoking gun, and the spring-back adds further support. I can see from this that this internal heating lead to failure of the wire splice.", "will_print_thinking": true}, "timestamp": 1767661285580}
