ygen internal heating, combined with the strand spring-back, strongly suggests resistance heating. That localized destruction of the splice, compared to the outer sheath, reinforces that it originated at the connection itself.\n\nVisually, the reddish/orange discoloration, characteristic of cuprous oxide, is evident within the splice. The wires exhibit a clear 'spring-back' effect as well. The insulation has clearly ruptured as a result of the high internal heat.\n\nMy verdict is **Poor Contact** causing the fire. I'm about 90% confident. The spring-back effect implies a heat build-up and resulting expansion/contraction, not simple melting from an external fire. The reddish cuprous oxide, formed in the oxygen-restricted environment of the tape, is classic evidence of electrical overheating.", "will_print_thinking": true}, "timestamp": 1767661527486}
