ey.\n\n*   **Causal Inference:** The \"spring-back\" is classic – it occurs when repeated high-temperature cycles from poor electrical contact cause the copper to lose its tension. The cuprous oxide? That's the smoking gun, literally. It forms under high-temperature conditions in an oxygen-deficient environment, like that contained within the taped splice. This indicates *internal* heating. An external fire, in contrast, would result in black cupric oxide on the *surface* and the twisted shape would generally be maintained unless physically disturbed. This evidence indicates a high resistance internal spark source and internal heating.\n\n*   **My Conclusion:** Based on everything, I have to conclude this fire was caused by **Poor Contact (접촉 불량)**. Specifically, localized overheating within the splice, leading to thermal runaway. The evidence, the cuprous oxide and spring-back, points overwhelmingly in that direction. I'm feeling very confident – about 90% sure, maybe even higher given the quality of the evidence. I'd give it a confidence level of 92. The internal localized high heat is the source of this fire, internal oxidation, not external fire.", "will_print_thinking": true}, "timestamp": 1767661941235}
